### Step 1: Find the angle between \(\vec{PQ}\) and \(\vec{PS}\).

Let \(\theta\) be the angle between \(\vec{PQ}\) and \(\vec{PS}\).
\cos\theta = \frac{\vec{PQ} \cdot \vec{PS}}{|\vec{PQ}||\vec{PS}|}
Given \(\vec{PQ} = \hat{j} + \hat{k}\) and \(\vec{PS} = \hat{i} - \hat{j}\).
\vec{PQ} = 0\hat{i} + 1\hat{j} + 1\hat{k}
\vec{PS} = 1\hat{i} - 1\hat{j} + 0\hat{k}
\vec{PQ} \cdot \vec{PS} = (0)(1) + (1)(-1) + (1)(0) = -1
|\vec{PQ}| = \sqrt{0^2 + 1^2 + 1^2} = \sqrt{2}
|\vec{PS}| = \sqrt{1^2 + (-1)^2 + 0^2} = \sqrt{2}
\cos\theta = \frac{-1}{\sqrt{2} \cdot \sqrt{2}} = \frac{-1}{2}
\theta = \frac{2\pi}{3}

### Step 2: Determine the angle \(\alpha\).

The side PS is rotated by an acute angle \(\alpha\) such that it becomes perpendicular to PQ. This means the new angle between the rotated PS and PQ is \(\frac{\pi}{2}\).
Since \(\alpha\) is an acute angle and the rotation is in the plane of the parallelogram, the angle between the rotated PS and PQ is \(\theta - \alpha\).
\theta - \alpha = \frac{\pi}{2}
\alpha = \theta - \frac{\pi}{2} = \frac{2\pi}{3} - \frac{\pi}{2} = \frac{4\pi - 3\pi}{6} = \frac{\pi}{6}

### Step 3: Calculate the required expression.

We need to find the value of \(\sin^2\left(\frac{5\alpha}{2}\right) - \sin^2\left(\frac{\alpha}{2}\right)\).
Using the trigonometric identity \(\sin^2 A - \sin^2 B = \sin(A+B)\sin(A-B)\).
Here, \(A = \frac{5\alpha}{2}\) and \(B = \frac{\alpha}{2}\).
A+B = \frac{5\alpha}{2} + \frac{\alpha}{2} = \frac{6\alpha}{2} = 3\alpha
A-B = \frac{5\alpha}{2} - \frac{\alpha}{2} = \frac{4\alpha}{2} = 2\alpha
So, the expression becomes \(\sin(3\alpha)\sin(2\alpha)\).
Substitute \(\alpha = \frac{\pi}{6}\):
\sin\left(3 \cdot \frac{\pi}{6}\right) \sin\left(2 \cdot \frac{\pi}{6}\right)
= \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right) \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{3}\right)
= 1 \times \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}
= \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}

questions.question:
Consider the matrices
A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & -2 \\ 4 & -2 \end{bmatrix}
and
B = \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 9 \\ 1 & 3 \end{bmatrix}
If matrices P and Q are such that \(PA = B\) and \(AQ = B\), then the absolute value of the sum of the diagonal elements of \(2(P + Q)\) is _______.

questions.explanation:
### Step 1: Calculate the inverse of matrix A.

Given matrix \(A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & -2 \\ 4 & -2 \end{bmatrix}\).
The determinant of A is \(\det(A) = (2)(-2) - (-2)(4) = -4 + 8 = 4\).
The adjoint of A is \(\text{adj}(A) = \begin{bmatrix} -2 & 2 \\ -4 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\).
So, the inverse of A is:
A^{-1} = \frac{1}{\det(A)}\text{adj}(A) = \frac{1}{4}\begin{bmatrix} -2 & 2 \\ -4 & 2 \end{bmatrix} = \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 1 \\ -2 & 1 \end{bmatrix}

### Step 2: Calculate matrix P.

Given \(PA = B\). To find P, multiply both sides by \(A^{-1}\) from the right:
P = BA^{-1}
P = \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 9 \\ 1 & 3 \end{bmatrix} \cdot \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 1 \\ -2 & 1 \end{bmatrix}
P = \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} (3)(-1) + (9)(-2) & (3)(1) + (9)(1) \\ (1)(-1) + (3)(-2) & (1)(1) + (3)(1) \end{bmatrix}
P = \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} -3 - 18 & 3 + 9 \\ -1 - 6 & 1 + 3 \end{bmatrix} = \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} -21 & 12 \\ -7 & 4 \end{bmatrix}

### Step 3: Calculate matrix Q.

Given \(AQ = B\). To find Q, multiply both sides by \(A^{-1}\) from the left:
Q = A^{-1}B
Q = \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 1 \\ -2 & 1 \end{bmatrix} \cdot \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 9 \\ 1 & 3 \end{bmatrix}
Q = \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} (-1)(3) + (1)(1) & (-1)(9) + (1)(3) \\ (-2)(3) + (1)(1) & (-2)(9) + (1)(3) \end{bmatrix}
Q = \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} -3 + 1 & -9 + 3 \\ -6 + 1 & -18 + 3 \end{bmatrix} = \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} -2 & -6 \\ -5 & -15 \end{bmatrix}

### Step 4: Calculate the sum of diagonal elements of \(2(P+Q)\).

First, find \(P+Q\):
P+Q = \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} -21 & 12 \\ -7 & 4 \end{bmatrix} + \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} -2 & -6 \\ -5 & -15 \end{bmatrix}
P+Q = \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} -21-2 & 12-6 \\ -7-5 & 4-15 \end{bmatrix} = \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} -23 & 6 \\ -12 & -11 \end{bmatrix}
Now, find \(2(P+Q)\):
2(P+Q) = 2 \cdot \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} -23 & 6 \\ -12 & -11 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -23 & 6 \\ -12 & -11 \end{bmatrix}
The diagonal elements of \(2(P+Q)\) are -23 and -11.
The sum of the diagonal elements (trace) is \(\text{tr}(2(P+Q)) = -23 + (-11) = -34\).
The absolute value of the sum of the diagonal elements is \(|-34| = 34\).
